A||||7||Lynx||Auto trans Performance||||5
Z||000000||Lynx||10-01-1999||04:32 AM||||Ok the only reason i want to pull the V6 out of this 85GT i got and put it into my 85 SC is beacause the GT is a automatic. Everywhere i read the auto is WAY slow and the top speed is like 10mph lower. Is there a way to help out the TH125? Is there another automatic trans that will bolt in its place that is better? I hear a computer chip will help auto performance but how much? Also last thing which i think is about impossible can you swap a auto for a manual? I know the swap would take a donor vehicle but how hard would it be?<P>Thanks for any answers anyone can provide.||||reg||5
Z||000001||mwbackus||10-01-1999||08:15 AM||||     A torque converter with a stall of around 2000 - 2500 rpm should get you off line performance that would scare any manual trans Fiero.  This will have negative effects on a "daily driver", so a line lock may be more preferable.  A shift kit and some good proven performance mods should make up for the small decrease in hp caused by the auto trans.  Theres not alot of computer control on the TH125 (other than the TCC) so a chip would have only slight benefits (if any). <P>     If you have the facilities to pull an engine from a Fiero, swapping in a manual trans would not be all that hard.  The clutch system and shift linkage would be the biggest issues.  A "manual" version of the chip for the ECM would also be needed.  Hmm..I wonder if you could use the trans cooler as an oil cooler?<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000002||Lynx||10-01-1999||12:30 PM||||So mainly the 0-60 times are so much slower for the auto is because of off the line performance right? Also if thats true then why are the top speeds lower? Also what in the heck is a line lock?||||reg||3
Z||000003||mwbackus||10-01-1999||01:37 PM||||No, an auto trans uses approx 10% more hp than a manual trans due to its design, they also weigh more, and the gear ratios are different.  But, if you make up for this horsepowere loss, you will be somewhat closer to the performance of a manual.  Shifting is actually faster, and with the addtional control and enhancement provided by a shift kit you can narrow this advantage significantly.  A line lock is simply an electronically controlled hydraulic brake valve operated by an rpm switch.  The whole idea behind a high stall converter, or a line lock is to launch at a higher rpm.  A high stall converter is much the equivalent of poping a clutch out at 2k or 3k rpm.  A line lock is more comparable to holding the brake on while abruptly increasing rpm. A line lock can be somewhat more detrimental to the drivetrain and tires than a high stall converter, but it may be much more desirable on a "daily driver" due to its "ondemand" functionality. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||10-02-1999||02:37 AM||||The function of a line lock is to hold the front brakes on while you roast'em.Was sold as a "hill holder" by Hurst a few years ago.<BR>I've seen torque convertors that allow an improved launch,yet don't hurt the daily use too badly.Mercedes V8s use something that spins to around 1900rpm,still goes(moves the car) before that speed with light throttle,only when you hammer it does it hook up at the higher rpm.You don't have to get the badboy,3000 stall convertor to get an improvement.For the money,I'd be tempted to try a 4 cyl convertor(convertors for larger engines are "tighter",to transfer the torque;convertor from a smaller engine will almost always give a higher stall speed). ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Lynx||10-02-1999||02:41 AM||||Thanks for the info and i will stay with the automatic and put a shift kit in it. Ill see about the line lock but it may be difficult to install. Does anyone know any thing about the Crane Compu Cams for the Fiero. I was looking at the cams from the Fiero store and there are three performance ones. It shows that on and two others. The others are 443lift,280duration and 480lift,300duration. Then they have the compu cam wich is more moaney and is said to work well with your computer but says no specs.||||reg||6
Z||000006||batboy||10-02-1999||10:38 AM||||For cams and other engine performance parts, it's hard to beat the prices of JEGS  <A HREF="http://www.jegs.com" TARGET=_blank>www.jegs.com</A>  or Performance Automotive Warehouse (PAW) 818-998-600. Both carry two versions of the Crane ComuCam.<P>The first CompuCam is designed for good low and mid range torque: duration is 258/264 and lift is .401/.423. This cam is the one listed at the Fiero Store as being a good pick for automatics.<P>The second CompuCam has a little less low end torque but more mid and high end HP: duration is 264/274 and lift is .423/.423. This is a good performance cam that works well with the computer.<P>The GM performance cam probably cost a little more, but it's specs are almost identical to the second CompuCam except lift on the exhaust valves are higher. The intake ports on a V6 flow better than the exhaust ports, so a little extra duration and lift on the exhaust valves seem to help create power.<P>I would NOT get that 300 degree advertised by the Fiero Store. It kills low end torque and will be very marginal in working with the computer. The top horsepower for the 300 degree cam really isn't anymore than the GM hipo cam. I'd go with the second Crane cam or better yet, the GM hipo cam.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||10-02-1999||10:47 AM||||Oh yeah, one more thing.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> In my opinion, line locks are a waste of money on a street driven car. That's purely drag racing stuff or used to spin the tires down to the rims doing a burnout. I have an automatic in my V8 and I have no problem running the engine up to 2,000 rpm using the regular brakes in order to launch from a stop. You don't want to spin the tires, that don't win races or get you down the road any faster.||||reg||1
